reviewer_comments.reviewer_2:
This is a well-executed study that makes significant contributions to biocatalysis and natural product synthesis. The authors successfully engineered AtaPT variants with dramatically improved activity and selectivity for flavonoid prenylation, identified a novel aromatic cage mechanism governing selectivity, and demonstrated practical applications through preparative-scale synthesis.
The work merits publication after addressing the following points.
Major Points:
1. Mechanistic Clarity: While the aromatic cage concept is compelling, the manuscript would benefit from:
a.More detailed explanation of why the "reverse" 8-prenylation occurs with M8 versus normal prenylation;
b. Quantitative analysis of substrate-cage interactions through computational binding energies;
c. Discussion of how the cage mechanism relates to other prenyltransferases beyond AtaPT.
2. Structure-Function Analysis:
a. The crystal structures show relatively weak electron density for substrate 1a. Additional validation through anomalous scattering or co-crystallization with substrate analogs would strengthen structural conclusions;
b. MD simulations are mentioned but insufficiently detailed - include RMSD plots, interaction frequencies, and convergence analysis;
3. Substrate Scope Limitations:
a. The inability to prenylate substrates 1t and 1u is attributed to missing hydroxyl groups, but this hypothesis needs experimental validation;
b. Test additional substrates with varied hydroxylation patterns to establish clearer structure-activity relationships.
4.Scale-up Considerations:
a. While 400 mg/L titers are reported, provide space-time yields and total turnover numbers;
b. Discuss enzyme stability over extended reaction times;
c. Address the relatively low conversion (15.8%) with in situ GPP generation compared to direct addition.
Minor Points:
1. Methods Section:
a. Include detailed protocols for the high-density fermentation mentioned;
b. Clarify the criteria for selecting mutation sites in directed evolution;
2. Data Presentation:
a. Figure 2d would benefit from error bars on all data points;
b. Include a supplementary table comparing kinetic parameters of all variants with all tested substrates;
c. Provide NMR spectra for all newly reported compounds in the SI;
3. Writing and Clarity:
a. Define abbreviations (SSM, ISM, FRISM) at first use in the main text;
b. The introduction could be condensed to focus more on the specific challenges addressed;
c. Clarify the distinction between "reverse" and normal prenylation earlier in the text.
4. Comparative Analysis:
a. Include a table comparing your results with other reported prenyltransferases for the same substrates;
b. Discuss how your aromatic cage mechanism relates to selectivity mechanisms in other enzyme families.
5. Practical Applications:
a. Discuss potential pharmaceutical applications of the newly synthesized compounds;
b. Address the cost-effectiveness of the biocatalytic approach versus chemical synthesis;
c. Comment on the environmental benefits of your approach.
6. Statistical Analysis:
a. Specify the number of independent experiments for all data;
b. Include statistical significance tests when comparing variants;
Technical Corrections:
a. Page 5: "Folk" should be defined (farnesol kinase?);
b. Figure 3 caption: Specify the resolution of the crystal structures;
c. Supplementary Table 6 reference appears before proper introduction;
d. Ensure consistent use of compound numbering throughout.
Strengths of the manuscript:
1. Novel mechanistic insights into prenyltransferase selectivity.
2. Impressive 45-fold improvement in catalytic efficiency.
3. Comprehensive substrate scope analysis.
4. Strong structural biology support for mechanistic conclusions.
5. Practical demonstration through preparative-scale synthesis.
This manuscript represents a significant advance in biocatalytic prenylation and provides valuable mechanistic insights. With the suggested revisions, particularly strengthening the mechanistic discussion and providing additional validation of the aromatic cage concept, this work would make an excellent contribution to Nature Communications. The combination of directed evolution, structural biology, and practical synthesis makes this a comprehensive study that will be of broad interest to the biocatalysis and natural products communities.

