### Reviewer 1

This work by Professor Wang and co-workers report the directed evolution of AtaPT, structural analysis and MD simulation of its variants and scalable synthesis of 26 C-prenylated flavonoids its variants. Especially, they found an aromatic cage governing both regioselectivity and donor specificity. This work expands the application the reported aromatic prenyltransferase AtaPT, and establishes a selective and scalable biocatalytic platform for flavonoid prenylation. It’s a very comprehensive work and achieved numerous results, thus it’s recommended to be accepted after the author address the following issues.
1, In Introduction Section, AtaPT should be much more introduced, such as its catalytic properties, and so on. Thus, the reader can understand the advances in this work.
2, Different from most prenylated products, the prenyl group in 2a was reverse instead of normal, why? Please give an explanation.
3, Page 8: the authors stated: “Notably, neither WT nor the tested mutants (G326W, M5, M7, and M8) exhibited activity toward substrates 1t and 1u, likely due to the absence of hydroxyl groups on the A and B rings, which are presumed to be critical for substrate recognition and binding.” In view of the reaction mechanism, this deduction isn’t completely corrected.
4, In Fig. 2: Considering easy-understanding for readers, HPLC chromatograms for the conversions of 1a by M8 and 1a by M7 should be provided here.
5, The steady-state kinetic constants of AtaPT variants, such as Km = 1.271 mM, it should be written as Km = 1.2 or Km = 1.3. Km = 1.271 mM seems to be strange, and imply an accuracy that will not be reproducible.
6, In scalable biocatalytic synthesis, why did the authors synthesized the compounds shown in Figure 5? Please give more description on these compounds, such as biological activities, etc.

